{
  "requirement": "Each bundled skill's description MUST state when to use it in terms of the user's task. It MAY name another bundled skill to use instead where an agent could confuse the two. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Site query.** I ran `requirements.cli.skill_conventions/skills`. It selects exactly the five `assets/skills/*/SKILL.md` files: `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-refactor`. A search for `^name: ` across the repo finds only those five skill files plus unrelated CI workflow names, so the query neither misses a bundled skill nor selects anything out of scope. The `references/` files under `assets/skills` carry no front matter, so they are not selected. The match snippet (front matter followed by a heading) shows what is selected. The no_match snippet (a heading with no front matter) shows the nearby text that must not be selected. Together they are adequate.\n\n**Obligations and tests.** The requirement has two testable obligations:\n1. Each description MUST state when to use the skill in terms of the user's task.\n2. A description MAY name another bundled skill to use instead. This is optional, so it needs no test.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skill_descriptions_say_when_to_use_them]` carries the `{test}` reference and checks both.\n- **Per skill.** For every name in `SKILLS`, it parses the front matter through `front_matter`. It asserts a single `name` equal to the skill name and a single non-empty `description` of at most 1024 characters. It also asserts the description equals what `description()` returns.\n- **When to use.** For all five skills, it asserts the description starts with a task-oriented \"Use …\" phrase. Examples are \"Use when adopting Habeas in an existing codebase\" and \"Use when a change moves code, tests, or requirements\". This covers the MUST. A skill with a missing or non-\"when\" description would fail.\n- **Named skills.** Any `habeas-*` word in a description must be a bundled skill in `SKILLS`. This checks that a skill named as an alternative really exists.\n- **Redirects.** It asserts the redirects for the confusable pairs, such as `habeas-requirements` pointing to `habeas-refactor` and `habeas-evidence`. It also asserts `habeas-evidence` and `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` point to their alternatives. These checks go beyond the optional MAY but are consistent with it.\n\n**Coverage of the skill set.** `SKILLS` is pinned to the actual `assets/skills` directories by `bundled_skills_are_five_skill_directories_each_home_to_its_topics`. A new skill without front matter or a description would therefore be caught.\n\n**Gaps.** I found no gap in the tests, the site query or its snippets.",
  "references": "4fbf9a3072386612109271b7cf55673834816c2318c1c11463d91326147378e5",
